//! # Expressions and control flow
//!
//! Hew uses expressions for values and statements for effects. The last
//! expression in a block is its value; adding a semicolon makes it a statement
//! with unit value. `if` and `match` can therefore return values directly.
//!
//! ```hew
//! enum Reading {
//!     Value(i64);
//!     Missing;
//! }
//!
//! fn describe(reading: Reading) -> string {
//!     match reading {
//!         .Value(value) if value > 80 => "high",
//!         .Value(_) => "normal",
//!         .Missing => "missing",
//!     }
//! }
//!
//! fn main() {
//!     var total = 0;
//!     for value in 1..=4 {
//!         if value % 2 == 0 {
//!             total += value;
//!         }
//!     }
//!     println(f"{describe(.Value(total))}: {total}");
//! }
//! ```
//!
//! ## Bindings and assignment
//!
//! `let` creates an immutable binding and `var` creates a mutable one. Use
//! `var` when reassigning the binding, assigning through one of its fields or
//! elements, or calling a `var self` method. Compound assignments include
//! `+=`, `-=`, `*=`, and `/=`. Hew does not use `let mut`.
//!
//! ## Branches and patterns
//!
//! `match` must cover every value. Enum matches can omit a wildcard so the
//! compiler reports newly added variants. Patterns can bind tuple positions,
//! enum payloads, and record fields; `if let` and `while let` use the same
//! pattern language when only one case matters. A guard follows a pattern as
//! `pattern if condition => value`; guarded arms do not count as exhaustive.
//! Conditions may chain boolean expressions and `let` patterns with `&&`.
//! `let pattern = value else { ... };` binds a successful pattern for the
//! surrounding block; its `else` branch must leave that block.
//!
//! ## Loops and ranges
//!
//! `for value in start..end` excludes `end`; `start..=end` includes it.
//! `while condition` repeats while its condition is true, and `loop` repeats
//! until `break`, `return`, or failure leaves it. `break` and `continue` work
//! in `for`, `while`, and `loop` bodies. Prefix a loop with a label such as
//! `@outer:` and write `break @outer` or `continue @outer` to target it from a
//! nested loop.
//!
//! ## Cleanup
//!
//! `defer { ... }` runs at the end of its block in last-in, first-out order.
//! It is for local synchronous cleanup; it cannot suspend. Prefer
//! [resources](language.resources.html) when a value owns an external lifetime,
//! because resource cleanup also follows early returns, failures, and task
//! cancellation.
